1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,745
Every month, small charges leave your
account.

2
00:00:03,745 --> 00:00:05,927
A free trial that never ended.

3
00:00:05,927 --> 00:00:08,109
A plan that crept up in price.

4
00:00:08,109 --> 00:00:13,418
The same service billed twice, once on your
card and once through PayPal.

5
00:00:13,418 --> 00:00:18,291
On a statement full of coffee and groceries,
none of it stands out.

6
00:00:18,291 --> 00:00:19,600
SubSweep finds it.

7
00:00:20,400 --> 00:00:22,639
I drop in a statement CSV.

8
00:00:22,639 --> 00:00:26,255
It can come from a bank, a card or PayPal.

9
00:00:26,255 --> 00:00:33,487
SubSweep detects the format and cleans up
the messy merchant names, and it all stays

10
00:00:33,487 --> 00:00:34,951
on this computer.

11
00:00:34,951 --> 00:00:39,600
The only thing ever saved is which findings
I dismiss.

12
00:00:40,400 --> 00:00:46,290
Here is the picture: ten subscriptions,
about seventeen hundred dollars a year, and

13
00:00:46,290 --> 00:00:48,348
a health score of forty-five.

14
00:00:48,348 --> 00:00:52,252
This is not machine learning, it is rules
you can read.

15
00:00:52,252 --> 00:00:58,426
Open Netflix: every charge on a chart, the
step up where the price rose, and the reason

16
00:00:58,426 --> 00:00:59,987
SubSweep is confident.

17
00:00:59,987 --> 00:01:04,600
Groceries and fuel are excluded, and it says
which rule did that.

18
00:01:05,400 --> 00:01:06,518
Now the findings.

19
00:01:06,518 --> 00:01:11,779
Streamly is billed twice: ten dollars a
month and a hundred a year, both through

20
00:01:11,779 --> 00:01:12,239
PayPal.

21
00:01:12,239 --> 00:01:17,960
The explanation is in plain English, and I
can see exactly what data it was built from.

22
00:01:17,960 --> 00:01:23,221
Here the wording is built in; with an
Anthropic key, Claude writes it, and every

23
00:01:23,221 --> 00:01:25,917
figure is still checked against the data.

24
00:01:25,917 --> 00:01:29,600
Keep one, and that saves about a hundred
dollars a year.

25
00:01:30,400 --> 00:01:31,971
PayPal adds ground truth.

26
00:01:31,971 --> 00:01:37,503
FitPass Gym shows as cancelled in PayPal on
the eleventh of August, yet my statement has

27
00:01:37,503 --> 00:01:39,891
two more charges, forty-eight dollars.

28
00:01:39,891 --> 00:01:42,720
A bank statement alone could never show
that.

29
00:01:42,720 --> 00:01:44,417
SubSweep only reads status.

30
00:01:44,417 --> 00:01:48,503
It cannot cancel anything, it links me to
PayPal so I act myself.

31
00:01:48,503 --> 00:01:53,531
This panel is labelled demo data; with
sandbox credentials the same screen reads

32
00:01:53,531 --> 00:01:54,600
live from PayPal.

33
00:01:55,400 --> 00:01:58,129
Let me act on the duplicate.

34
00:01:58,129 --> 00:02:05,634
SubSweep drafts a short, polite message,
with placeholders for my own account

35
00:02:05,634 --> 00:02:06,413
details.

36
00:02:06,413 --> 00:02:08,070
It is never sent.

37
00:02:08,070 --> 00:02:11,969
There is a copy button, and that is all.

38
00:02:11,969 --> 00:02:14,600
I decide what happens next.

39
00:02:15,400 --> 00:02:18,054
And I can ask my own data a question.

40
00:02:18,054 --> 00:02:20,133
What do I spend on streaming?

41
00:02:20,133 --> 00:02:25,656
Twenty-six dollars a month, Hulu and
Netflix, with the data used right there.

42
00:02:25,656 --> 00:02:28,811
SubSweep: find what is quietly draining you.

43
00:02:28,811 --> 00:02:29,600
You decide.
